into the mechanics let's  lay out the main principles  a game needs to include multiple players  the players need to interact with each  other there needs to be a reward we  assume that players act rationally we  assume that players act according to  their personal self-interest a skeptical  person would automatically assume that  these rules can be applied to real life  since most people operate in a strictly  emotional and irrational manner stay  with me for a second let's dive a bit  deeper and you will open your eyes to  probably the most important decision  making theory ever conceived  prisoner's dilemma is the most widely  mentioned game in game theory the basic  premise is how to establish a mutually  beneficial strategy between two members  of a gang that got arrested and face  potential imprisonment the rules are as  follows  the players of the game are prisoner a  and prisoner b  the players can't communicate with each  other if a and b each betray each other  each of them serves two years in prison  if a betrays b but b remains silent a  will be set free and b will serve three  years in prison and vice versa  if a and b both remain silent both of  them will only serve one year in prison  this matrix depicts the different  choices and its number signifies the  number of years to serve according to  each choice since both members can't  interact with each other the obvious  mutually beneficial choice here is to  betray choosing to remain silent  constitutes a high risk choice since the  other party could betray and the party  that remains silent will serve 3 years  in prison betraying sounds a bit cruel  but in this specific game it is optimal  stay with me and i will explain in a bit  how this strategy can actually change  game theorists usually coin the terms  dominant strategy and nas equilibrium to  distinguish the kind of strategies  followed by the players these two terms  are usually used together and form the  basis upon which game theory is founded  dominant strategy refers to strategies  that are better than other strategies  for one player no matter how the  opponent may play in the prisoner's  dilemma case the dominant strategy for  its player is to betray  the term nash equilibrium is used since  john nass explained the essentiality of  equilibrium and his landmark work  equilibrium points in any person games  in essence what he proposed is that even  in high-level competitive games like  google versus apple or usa versus russia  there exists an equilibrium where no  side would benefit by changing its  course at this equilibrium its side  knows its adversary very well and sticks  to its strategy for instance in the  prisoner's dilemma the nas equilibrium  is the bottom right square of the matrix  both prisoners to betray  what makes nasa's theory of equilibrium  so special is that it presupposes that  in each game there is at least one point  of equilibrium and all players will be  better off trying to find it and form  their strategy around it  this momentous revelation help people in  disciplines like politics war economics  business and social theory understand  the world better and form better  strategies the inception of nas  equilibrium was portrayed falsely in the  wonderful movie a beautiful mind  deep breaths  yes you might want to stop shuffling  your papers for five seconds  i will not buy you gentlemen beer oh  we're not here for beer my friend  oh [ __ ]  does anyone else feel she should be  moving in slow motion  will she want a large wedding you think  should we say swords gentlemen pistols  at dawn have you remembered nothing  recall the lessons of adam smith the  father of modern economics in uh in  competition individual ambition serves  the common good exactly every man for  himself gentlemen and those who strike  out are stuck with their friends  i'm not gonna strike out you can lead a  blonde to water but you can't make a  drink i don't think you said that all  right nobody move  she's looking over again she's looking  at nash oh god  all right he may have the upper hand now  but wait until he opens his mouth  [Music]  adam smith needs revision what are you  talking about  if we all go for the blonde  [Music]  we block each other not a single one of  us  is going to get her  so then we go for her friends  [Music]  but they will all give us the cold  shoulder because nobody likes to be  second choice  but what if no one goes for the blood  we don't get in each other's way  and we don't insult the other girls  that's the only way we win  that's the only way we all get laid  while this scene wants to point out the  importance of cooperation it's not an  example of nash equilibrium a nasa  equilibrium is only reached when all  players adopt a strategy where no single  player by changing his or her strategy  can obtain a better outcome in the  example shown in the movie that's not  the case assuming that everyone prefers  the blonde  any one of the guys could independently  change strategy by going for her  therefore if no one else goes after her  she could accept him and he could be the  only one ending up with her hence the  proposed strategy is not a nash  equilibrium and nash equilibrium is  really only obtained when all men follow  the strategy of going after the blonde  according to wikipedia researchers found  out that if two players play prisoners  dilemma more than once in succession and  they remember previous actions of their  opponent and changed their strategy  accordingly greedy strategies tended to  do very poorly in the long run while  more altruistic strategies did better  the researchers used this to show a  possible mechanism for the evolution of  altruistic behavior from mechanisms that  are initially purely selfish by natural  selection the essence of game theory is  that it looks at strategic decision  making and offers insight into the  dynamics of human rivalry apparently  even if people or companies rationally  follow their own self-interest the best  outcome is hard to reach when they count  or don't cooperate therefore the best  is for one to continually scan the  market for potential alliances and keep  playing games so that one can understand  them well that's what i love about game  theory it approaches the game of life  from its most axiomatic principle that  of evolution evolution equals adaptation  and therefore life is an omnipresent  game of adaptation humans always compete  at first but given the right  circumstances humans will choose  cooperation because it is the main  strategy that helped us survive and  thrive after years of adversity and  struggle to compete against different  forces of nature i always believed that  change and equilibrium start at the  micro level  and that is something we fail to grasp  because we are usually consumed by  events at the macro what each of us  could do is to use game theory in our  most challenging decisions and in our  closest relationships regardless of the  circumstance try to think like a game  theorist ponder questions like are the  actors in the situation rational are we  able to reach a nas equilibrium do they  act according to their self-interest do  they understand the rules of the game is  their dominant strategy really that  dominant if you see that most game  theory parameters aren't met you know  that you have to change your approach  you either need to also act irrationally  which is something i wouldn't suggest or  you need to step away and find better  players or games the choice is always  yours  life often times feels like a game and  we make the game better by learning how  to play  [Music]
 

